--TEST--
A `?->` chain cannot be written through in any of its spellings — a plain assignment, a compound one, an increment and a write to an element under it are all `E0479` — `docs/reference/lang/30-expressions.md` § `??`, `?:`, `?->` and the ternary
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: lang:expressions/and-the-ternary
// `?->` yields `null` when its receiver is `null`, and `null` is not a place
// to store anything in, so the refusal is about the whole family of writes
// and not about the one spelling that is easiest to reach. Each line below
// is a different way to write through the chain, and each has to lose its
// own error: a spelling that stops being checked would still look right
// beside the other three.
//
// The compound form reports three times. Its target is refused, the value it
// reads can be `null` and so has no `+`, and the sum then has no type left to
// store; the later errors are the consequence of the first and are expected
// here rather than filtered out.
class P {
    public int $x = 1;
    public array<int> $items = [];
}

?P $p = new P();

$p?->x = 2;
$p?->x += 1;
$p?->x++;
$p?->items["k"] = 3;
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0479]: `?->` cannot be written through
%A
error[E0479]: `?->` cannot be written through
%A
error[E0716]: `+` has no meaning for `int|null`
%A
error[E0401]: expected `int|null`, found `mixed`
%A
error[E0479]: `?->` cannot be written through
%A
error[E0479]: `?->` cannot be written through
%A
